WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:14.290
‘I pledge allegiance to the flag of the
United States of America, and to the republic

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:38.700
for which it stands, one nation under God,
indivisible, with liberty and justice for

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.190
all.’
[Music]

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:52.399
My name is Robert Rountree and I teach seventh
grade science in Pittsburgh, New York which

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:59.249
is upstate New York. And we focus on chemistry,
so I love to tell the kids that I love chemistry

00:00:59.249 --> 00:01:07.060
because I love the Periodic Table.
I remember my middle school teachers and I

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:13.190
remember the demonstrations, so when I started
teaching I kind of used that as a guide that

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:28.750
I would try to make sure that I would give
the kids as many demonstrations as possible.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:33.060
I think more times than not I use it as what’s
called a motivator, meaning you try to do

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.759
something…the research says the kids will
remember whatever you say first and whatever

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:42.070
you say last and if you’re lucky they will
remember something in-between. And that’s

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:46.450
always the trick of putting a lesson together,
but if you can grab their attention at the

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:51.390
beginning then you tend to have it for the
whole rest of the class. You know the idea

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:56.680
of having a video for each element is brilliant,
it really is, because that way in one place

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.390
you can go to and find out what it is you
want to know about that element and then each

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:05.510
video obviously leads to more and more questions.
As soon as a kid mentions an element, or I

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:10.570
mention an element, it will be like go to
the video.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:24.390
[Muted sound from video playing to the class]
Good Chemistry! Melt on!

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:41.120
‘The demo of the day is the demo of the
demo of the demo of the day!’ [Class cheers]

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:47.230
You know when Brady said he was coming I thought
well what can I do, I was thinking, and I

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:51.270
don’t know whether I had just seen the zinc
video or I went home and saw the zinc video,

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:55.520
but I just thought that would be a really
cool demonstration. You know because there’s

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:02.740
the ‘oh ah’ factor and then YouTube, is
it YouTube friendly? I decided in putting

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:06.870
this together that I better find out whether
it was legal or not otherwise I was going

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:10.220
to have to do something with dry ice, which
would have been awesome but not quite the

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:15.430
‘oh ah’ factor I was looking for. And
I called the USA mint and I got the person

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:19.610
who sells coins, and she thought it was an
awesome question, and she said well let me

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:24.970
give you some numbers and there is an educational
exemption that if you are melting pennies

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:37.120
for educational purposes that that in fact
is okay, it is legal. Is white hot hotter

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:43.540
than red hot?
Hi Professor my question is: is it aluminum

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.830
or aluminium?
What’s your favourite element?

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:52.810
What’s your least favourite element?
Hi, what’s the most dangerous element?

00:03:52.810 --> 00:04:02.110
What first got you interested in science?
So I just press space…or do I have to have

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:04.880
the thing on the…? They’re interesting
questions aren’t they?

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.980
Hey Professor, have you ever played with mercury?

